## Overview 

We completed a heart attack risk statistical analysis. Our objective was to help doctors better understand a patient’s heart attack risk. We wanted to build models to help predict risk and create a dashboard to visualize these predictions. This would allow a medical professional to be able to better understand the heart attack risk of a new patient based on their attributes.

Our primary research question was if clinical markers could accurately predict heart attack risk. A secondary objective was to see if a dashboard and rule-based scoring system could be an effective alternative for providing clinical insight when there is weak model performance.

## Data 

We used the "Heart Attack Risk Prediction Cleaned Dataset" from Kaggle. This dataset is designed for predicting heart attack risk. All numerical values were already normalized. There were 9,651 rows and 27 total columns. The dataset had already been cleaned with all data being in the same format, but we removed all rows with N/A. This reduced the data to 9,377 rows. We also made sure all binary variables were in the form of 0 or 1. The response variable we chose was "Heart Attack Risk (Binary)." 

## Exploratory Data Analysis

